<p><a href="/documentation">Documentation</a> / Browse groups</p>
<h1>Browse groups for a course</h1>
<p class="lead">
  Find the study groups associated with your selected course.
</p>

<h2>Before you begin</h2>
<p>
  <a href="/quickstart">Launch StudyHub</a> and
  <a href="/features/create-group">create an example group</a>
  if your list is empty. Groups exist only for the current session.
</p>

<h2>Step-by-step instructions</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Show all</strong> to display all current groups
      and clear previous filters.</li>
  <li>Open the <strong>Course</strong> dropdown.</li>
  <li>Select the course you want to browse, such as
      <strong>CS101</strong>.</li>
  <li>Review the resulting group list and the result count above it.
      Groups for other courses are hidden.</li>
  <li>Click a group to select it. To read its meeting information,
      click <strong>View details</strong>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Show all</strong> when you want to browse
      groups across all courses again.</li>
</ol>

<figure>
  <img src="/images/studyhub-courses.png"
       alt="StudyHub showing CS101 Study Crew after filtering the group list to CS101">
  <figcaption>
    The group list displays groups belonging to the selected course.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>If no groups appear</h2>
<p>
  The empty state means no current groups match your filters.
  Clear any text in <strong>Search code</strong> to see all groups
  for the selected course, or click <strong>Show all</strong>
  to reset both filters.
</p>
<p>
  If the course still has no groups, click <strong>Create group</strong>
  and select that course in the form.
</p>

<aside class="notice">
  <strong>Filters work together:</strong>
  When a course is selected and search text is entered, a group must
  match both conditions to appear.
</aside>

<p>
  Next: <a href="/features/view-details">View group details</a>
</p>

